  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Above normal temperatures and dry conditions will \nprevail through Friday. \n\nUpper level ridging near the Gulf will shift northeastward over the \nregion Thursday, as sfc high pressure lingers overhead. Thus, expect \ndry conditions and above normal temperatures to prevail for the \nremainder of the work week with the aid of sunny skies. Currently \nhave highs in the mid to upper 80s, which is 7-9 degrees above \nnormal for this time of year. A few locations may even take a shot \nat 90 degrees, which could put a few of our record highs in jeopardy \n(see Climate section below). Upper level pattern shifts heading into \nthe weekend, as a strong cold front dives across the central CONUS \nand into our area by Saturday. Temperatures during this time will be \nnotably cooler, as highs only rise into the upper 60s to low 70s. \nOvernight lows will also feel quite crisp, especially Sunday \nmorning, as values fall into the upper 40s to 50s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Expect gusty winds and temperatures well below normal \nthis weekend behind a strong cold front.\n\nA strong cold front will sweep across southeast Georgia and the SC \nLowcountry late Friday night into Saturday morning. Medium range \nguidance generally agrees that it should be a dry cold front, only \nsprinkles across portions of the CHS Tri-county late Friday night \ninto early Saturday morning. GFS H85 temperatures over Lake Moultrie \ncools by 10 degrees between 0Z Sat to 0Z Sun. As a result, \ntemperature will fall well below normal on Sat, with highs ranging \naround 70 degrees. In addition, north-northeast winds across the \nregion will be breezy, with gusts around 25 mph. \n\nSaturday night into Sunday, the center of a 1035 mb high will settle \nover VA by daybreak Sunday. The sfc high will ridge SW across the \nCarolinas and Georgia on Sunday. North winds will continue through \nthe period, generally between 10-15 mph with gusts between 20 to 25 \nmph. The north winds will maintain cool llvl thicknesses across the \nforecast area. Using a blend of guidance, low temperatures on Sunday \nare forecast to range from the upper 40s inland to the mid to upper \n50s along the coast. High temperatures are forecast to range from \naround 70 degrees across the Lowcountry to the low to mid 70s across \nSE GA. \n\nThe center of sfc high pressure should shift over off the New \nEngland coast on Monday, resulting in winds to weaken and shift from \nthe east. Temperatures across the CWA should warm to around \nseasonable normals, mid to upper 70s. Little change expected for \nTuesday.",
